module gunbc.repo_self_build


// THE SINGLE POINT THROUGH WHICH THIS REPOSITORY BUILDS ITS OWN BINARIES.
// Before this module the same operation was spelled independently in three
// workflow authorities and hand-authored in a fourth, with the bin sets
// differing and nothing relating them, so "how this repo builds its compiler"
// had four owners and could be changed inconsistently -- which it already had
// been. Operator ruling 2026-08-24: everything compiles through the same
// point, no forks allowed.
//
// The split follows DESIGN section 3. The cargo BUILD SHAPE is upstream's and
// belongs to extdeps.rust.cargo. WHICH package and WHICH bins is business
// policy and lives here. How a consumer transports it -- a workflow RunStep, a
// host effect, an interpreter service call -- is realization and lives with
// the consumer. A caller that writes the argv itself has taken all three back.
//
// The construction is not novel: witness_floor_workflow already folded a bin
// roster onto one base command, correctly, in private. What was missing was
// not the idea but its REACH -- three other consumers could not call it. This
// module is that function promoted to where every consumer can reach it.

data repo_self_build_authority_note: String = "Single authority for the argv this repository uses to build its own binaries. The package is a fact about THIS repository's layout, not about cargo, so it is data here rather than a parameter every caller re-supplies: a caller free to name the package could build a different crate and still look like a conforming call. The BIN SET is a parameter because it genuinely differs by consumer -- the required floor needs claim_executor and gunbc, fleet-desired needs gunbc alone -- and that difference is the only axis on which honest callers disagree. THE CARGO ARGV WORDS HERE ARE A REAL FORK AGAINST extdeps.rust.cargo_build AND CONSUMING IT IS NOT AVAILABLE, which review 55636 raised and which is recorded with its measurement so the next author does not attempt the drop-in and discover this alone. Every cargo argv word in that module -- \"cargo\", \"build\", \"-p\", \"--bin\" -- appears ONLY inside a transport shell block: measured on the live file, its entire declaration surface is one ExternalAuthority anchor, one env map, one note and two jobs-flag fns, and no data or fn exposes the vocabulary at all. So there is no expression a fn in this module could read it from, and the argv is fused to the shell transport that spells it. That is exactly the DESIGN section 3 interface-versus-realization seam: this consumer needs a workflow YAML STRING, which is a second realization of the same operation, so the correct repair is to DE-FUSE the operation shape from transport shell and bind both realizations to it -- not to have this module reach into a transport block, which is not a thing the language admits. The follow-up is therefore de-fusion in extdeps, strictly larger than this PR and correctly outside it; until it lands the fork stands, named rather than silent. SO THIS STRING RENDERER IS A BOUNDED WORKFLOW ADAPTER AND IS DECLARED AS ONE, not a second cargo authority claiming equal standing with the modeled surface. The split it adapts across is: cargo syntax and invocation shape belong to the extdeps cargo authority; repository package and bin policy belong here; workflow shell rendering belongs to the workflow realization. This module currently spans the second and third of those and borrows the first, which is precisely the boundary the de-fusion above closes. DISSOLUTION TRIGGER: this renderer dissolves when the cargo operation shape is de-fused from transport shell and can be bound to a workflow-text realization, at which point repo_self_build produces a TYPED cargo invocation carrying package and bins and a shared renderer lowers it -- and the argv words disappear from this module entirely rather than being spelled correctly here. Until then the correct reading of these fns is an adapter with a named terminus, and a reviewer should refuse any NEW argv word added here that the modeled surface already owns."

data repo_self_build_axes_deferred_note: String = "TWO AXES WERE BUILT HERE AND BACKED OUT BEFORE MERGE, recorded so the next author does not re-derive them from scratch. A CargoBuildSubject coproduct (RepositoryCrate | EmittedCandidateTree { manifest_path }) and a CargoVerb (CargoBuild | CargoCheck) were added for the self-host regen gate, which must cargo-check an EMITTED CANDIDATE TREE -- a different crate, which the package-as-data decision above structurally excludes -- and for which check suffices because it establishes the required compile diagnostics without paying for final binary production. THE COMPARISON THAT ESTABLISHED THAT IS NAMED RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBED, and an earlier revision of this note carried its wall times and peak memory directly. That was the measurement-bankruptcy violation the authority on main forbids in as many words -- name the instrument, never transcribe its output -- committed in a .dag authority by the same branch that ported the corrected rule into the design document, which is the sharpest available demonstration that a transcribed number is authored faster than the rule against it is remembered. The re-deriving producer is a cargo check against a cargo build of the same subject at the same head; the qualitative claim above is the enduring one and the magnitudes are a property of the run that measured them. Review 55633 called them speculative widening and was right: nothing in that diff consumed either axis, the sole caller pinned both to their defaults, and DESIGN section 6 names a new artifact with no final consumer as experimental residue. The same standard had just been applied one PR over, where an unwired affected-set lens was split out of this branch for exactly this reason; leaving these in would have been that principle applied to one artifact and not its neighbour. THE DESIGN STANDS AND IS NOT RE-OPENED BY THE BACK-OUT: a free package parameter is the wrong remedy because it discards the property the note above relies on, and a sibling authority sharing this argv vocabulary is the section 3 fork this module exists to prevent -- so the subject belongs here as a CLOSED vocabulary, and it lands in the diff that carries its consumer rather than ahead of it."

data repo_self_build_package: NonEmptyStr = "v1-compiler"

data repo_self_build_profile_flag: String = "--release"

data repo_self_build_empty_bins_note: String = "An empty bin set renders the package build with no --bin selector, which is cargo's own meaning for it (build every target in the package) and is a DIFFERENT request from building a named subset -- not a degenerate case of one. It is rendered rather than refused because a fold over an empty roster produces exactly this string in the construction this module promotes, so the state is reachable by an honest caller; a refusal here would break a caller that is asking cargo a legitimate question."

fn repo_self_build_bin_flag(bin: String) -> String {
  join(["--bin ", bin], "")
}

fn repo_self_build_bin_selector(bins: List<String>) -> String {
  join(bins |> map(b => repo_self_build_bin_flag(bin: b)), " ")
}

fn repo_self_build_package_command() -> String {
  join(
    ["cargo build", repo_self_build_profile_flag, "-p", repo_self_build_package as String],
    " "
  )
}

fn repo_self_build_command(bins: List<String>) -> String {
  if bins |> count == 0 {
    repo_self_build_package_command()
  } else {
    join([repo_self_build_package_command(), repo_self_build_bin_selector(bins: bins)], " ")
  }
}
